From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:43:33 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: sm5705_charger: follow a USB PD contract above
 5 V

The input limit comes from the MUIC's BC1.2 type, or from the USB-C
port when that allows more. A USB PD charger can be a BC1.2 dedicated
charging port as well: an Apple USB-C charger reads as DCP, 1.8 A,
which beats the 1.65 A the port allows at 9 V. With VBUS at 9 V the
charger then kept the 5 V DCP limit, did not count the source as 15 W,
and did not know that VBUS was high, which keeps the torch off it.

The BC1.2 limits are for 5 V. Let the port decide whenever its
contract is above 5.5 V.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820) with an Apple USB-C charger (PD
2.0, 5 V to 20 V) and a 9 V / 3 A contract: before, input limit
1.8 A, USB type DCP, "standard source"; after, input limit 1.65 A,
USB type PD, "15 W source, charging at up to 2700 mA".

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/power/supply/sm5705_charger.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/sm5705_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/sm5705_charger.c
index b23bf8cbc8..6e07b292a2 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/sm5705_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/sm5705_charger.c
@@ -646,10 +646,13 @@ static void sm5705_chg_cable_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		type = POWER_SUPPLY_USB_TYPE_UNKNOWN;
 	}
 
-	/* a USB-C source may allow more than its BC1.2 type says */
+	/*
+	 * a USB-C source may allow more than its BC1.2 type says, and the
+	 * BC1.2 limits are for 5 V: a PD contract above that decides
+	 */
 	typec_limit = sm5705_chg_typec_limit(chg, &typec_type, &typec_fast,
 					     &typec_hv, &usb_c);
-	if (typec_limit > limit) {
+	if (typec_limit > limit || typec_hv) {
 		type = typec_type;
 		limit = typec_limit;
 		fast = typec_fast;
